<section class="inner-sec">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">How it works in a chiro office</div>
  <h2>Every call answered, every visit kept</h2>
  <p class="lede">One receptionist covers the phone, the texts, the website and the socials — getting new patients booked and existing ones rescheduled, while your team stays in the adjusting room.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Missed-call text-back</strong>A call nobody could reach becomes a text conversation in seconds, instead of a voicemail your desk works through after close.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Answers the front-desk questions</strong>Hours, location, whether you're taking new patients, what a first visit involves, techniques you use — answered instantly, any hour.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>The insurance question, answered honestly</strong>It tells callers which plans you're in-network with and what a cash visit runs, then hands anything specific to your team. It never verifies benefits or promises coverage.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Books new patients</strong>New-patient calls go straight onto the schedule instead of into a callback pile — including the ones that come in at 9 PM.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Reschedules by text</strong>The "can I move Thursday?" message gets handled without anyone picking up a phone, so care plans stay intact.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Human handoff</strong>Anything clinical, anything sensitive, anything unusual goes to your staff with a summary of the conversation.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">Who it's built for</div>
  <h2>However your practice is set up</h2>
  <p class="lede">The front-desk load looks different in a solo office than in a three-provider clinic, and the receptionist is configured to match rather than assuming one shape.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Solo practice</strong>You're adjusting and answering. Every call you take is a patient waiting, and every one you don't is a patient lost.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Two providers, one front desk</strong>Double the schedule, same one person at the desk. The overflow is where patients go missing.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Cash practice</strong>No benefits to check, but far more price and plan questions up front — answered from what you've published, never invented.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Insurance-based</strong>The in-network question gets a straight answer, and anything needing real verification reaches a human.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Sports &amp; rehab focus</strong>Weekend injuries mean Sunday-night searches. Those calls land when the office is dark.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Family &amp; prenatal care</strong>Whether you adjust kids or expectant mothers is a common gate question, and one your website often doesn't answer clearly.</div>
  </div>
</section>

